WN.lb(29,{"mt_ocdr7SixgO":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A new friend asks, \"How old are you?\" What do you say?","choices":["I am 3.","I am Mia.","My gran."],"answer":0,"hint":"The friend wants a number of years.","explain":"\"I am 3\" tells your age. \"I am Mia\" is your name."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Sam is {n}. He has {n} candles. Is that right?","choices":["Yes, one for each year","No, he needs lots","No, he needs one"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count one candle for each year old.","explain":"Sam is {n}, so {n} candles is right: one candle for each year.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Mia lives with her mum and her gran. Is that a family?","choices":["Yes","No, she needs a dad","No, it is too small"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are all families the same?","explain":"Mia, her mum and her gran are a real family. Families are different."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which is a special thing about you?","choices":["I can hop.","Mum is tall.","It is sunny."],"answer":0,"hint":"Pick something about you, not someone else.","explain":"\"I can hop\" is about you. Mum being tall is about Mum."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Bo likes blue. You like red. Who is right?","choices":["Both of you","Only you","Only Bo"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it wrong to like a different colour?","explain":"Both of you are right. People like different things, and that is OK."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Who lives at your house? Which answer fits?","choices":["My mum and my dad.","I am 2.","I like cake."],"answer":0,"hint":"The question is about people at your home.","explain":"\"My mum and my dad\" says who lives with you."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"There are {n} people in Ava's family. In Leo's there are 3. Which is a real family?","choices":["Both of them","Only the big one","Only the small one"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the size of a family matter?","explain":"Big or small, both are real families: Ava's {n} and Leo's 3.","params":{"n":[4,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A friend tells you, \"I am Bo.\" What did Bo tell you?","choices":["His name","His age","Where he lives"],"answer":0,"hint":"\"I am\" and then a word like Bo, Mia or Sam.","explain":"\"I am Bo\" tells you his name."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Bo asks, \"What is your name?\" Which answer fits?","choices":["I am Mia.","I am 3.","Yes!"],"answer":0,"hint":"Bo wants to know what to call you.","explain":"\"I am Mia\" tells Bo your name. \"I am 3\" tells him your age."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Mia is {n}. How many candles go on her cake?","choices":["{n} candles","No candles","Lots and lots"],"answer":0,"hint":"One candle for each year old.","explain":"Mia is {n}, so she has {n} candles: one for each year.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Leo lives with his dad and his papa. Is that a family?","choices":["Yes, it is his family","No, he needs a mum","No, it is too small"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are all families the same?","explain":"Families are different. Leo, his dad and his papa are a real family."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Ava's home has {n} people and a dog. Is the dog part of her home?","choices":["Yes, pets can be","No, only people","Only if it is big"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who lives at Ava's home, every day?","explain":"The dog lives with Ava, so it is part of her home, along with the {n} people.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["You have a name. When a new friend asks, say it: \"I am Mia.\"","You can say how old you are. Hold up your fingers. One candle for each year on your cake.","Who lives with you? Mum, Dad, Gran, a brother or a cat. Every home is different, and every one is a family.","You are special. You can hop, sing or do puzzles. Your friend likes different things, and that is OK."],"adult":["This topic is an early step in self-awareness: a child of two or three learns to say their first name and age, to name the people who live with them or look after them, and to share one thing that is special about them. It feeds later work on feelings, friendships and belonging.","In the lab your child answers a friendly bear's three questions, puts one candle per year on three birthday cakes, visits three homes with different families, fills their own home with the people and pets who live there, and picks two special things for a star before deciding whether liking different colours makes anyone wrong.","Common mistakes: answering a different question from the one asked (\"I am 3\" for \"What is your name?\"); counting candles past the age; and the idea, often picked up from books, that a family must be a mum, a dad and children. Children whose homes look different can feel left out, so name many kinds of family. Avoid quizzing: a few questions in play works better than a test.","Practise with toys or a puppet: let the puppet ask your child's name and age, then let your child ask the puppet. Look at family photos and name everyone together."]},"familyTip":"Make an 'all about me' page together: draw or stick a photo of your child, write their name and age, add the people and pets at home, and one thing they love doing. Let them 'read' it to a grandparent or a teddy.","keyIdea":"You can tell people about you: your name, how old you are, who lives with you, and what makes you special. Every family is different. A family can be big or small, with a mum, a dad, two dads, a gran or a pet, and each one is a real family.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it about me? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["About me","Not about me"],"items":[["my name",0],["how old I am",0],["who lives with me",0],["what I can do",0],["the weather",1],["the colour of a bus",1],["what is on TV",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfam","caption":"Flagship lab: tell Bo your name and age, put candles on cakes, visit three different homes, fill your own home and make a star of what makes you special.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_9LzHabN_zX":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which one tells what you like?","choices":["I like bananas.","I am 4.","I can hop."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the words I like at the start.","explain":"I like bananas tells what you like. I am 4 tells your age, and I can hop tells what you can do."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which job needs a grown-up?","choices":["Use the oven","Wash my hands","Put on my coat"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is very hot and could burn you?","explain":"The oven is very hot, so a grown-up uses it. You can wash your hands and put on your coat."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You cannot reach your coat on the hook. What can you do?","choices":["Ask a grown-up to help","Climb on the shelf","Give up"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do you say when a job is too hard or not safe?","explain":"Ask: can you help me, please? Climbing is not safe, and asking for help is a smart choice."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Leo picks {n} toys he likes. Pip picks other toys. Is that OK?","choices":["Yes, friends can like different toys","No, Pip must copy Leo","No, they cannot play"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do friends have to like all the same things?","explain":"Yes. Leo likes his {n} toys and Pip likes others. They can still play together and share.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which can you do all by yourself?","choices":["Wash my hands","Cross a busy road","Take medicine"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which job is safe for you to do alone?","explain":"You can wash your hands: soap, rub, rinse, dry. Roads and medicine need a grown-up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Mia does not like cheese. What can she say?","choices":["No thanks","Yuck, you are bad","Throw it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the kind way to say you do not want it.","explain":"Mia can say no thanks. It is OK not to like a food, and no thanks is a kind way to say it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Ava and Pip both like {n} red balls. What can they say?","choices":["We both like balls","Only I like balls","Balls are bad"],"answer":0,"hint":"When you like the same thing, which word joins you both?","explain":"We both like balls. When friends like the same thing, they can play with the {n} balls together.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Mia likes peas. Leo does not like peas. Is that OK?","choices":["Yes, people like different things","No, Leo is wrong","No, Mia is wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is a like right or wrong, or does it just belong to you?","explain":"Yes. A like belongs to the person. Mia and Leo can like different foods and still be friends."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Ava has {n} toys. Which job can she do by herself?","choices":["Tidy her toys","Use the oven","Cross the road"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which job is not hot, fast or sharp?","explain":"Ava can tidy all {n} toys into the box by herself. The oven and the road need a grown-up.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You like trains. Pip likes cars. What can you say?","choices":["I like trains but Pip likes cars.","Pip is wrong.","I like cars."],"answer":0,"hint":"Say your like first, then Pip's like, with but in the middle.","explain":"I like trains but Pip likes cars. That says both likes and is kind to Pip."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Sam pours water for {n} friends. The cup is full to the line. What next?","choices":["Stop pouring","Pour more","Shake the cup"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens to water that goes past the line?","explain":"Stop at the line, or it spills. Then Sam can pour the next cup for his {n} friends.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["What food do you like? Say it out loud. I like apples! You can say no thanks too. That is OK.","Your friend may like other things. You like trains. Pip likes cars. You both like the ball. Same and different is fine.","You can do lots by yourself. You can put on your coat. You can wash your hands. You can tidy your toys.","Some jobs need a grown-up. The oven is hot. Roads have fast cars. Then you say, can you help me, please?"],"adult":["At three and four, children are building a sense of self: what they like, what they are good at, and what they can manage alone. Early years guidance calls this self-awareness and growing independence in meeting their own care needs, such as washing hands and putting on a coat.","Saying a preference out loud is a real skill. Children learn that a like is not right or wrong, that a friend can like something different, and that they can still play together. The sentence frame 'I like... but my friend likes...' gives them words for this.","Common mistakes to watch for: a child thinks a friend is wrong for liking something different; a child copies the friend's answer instead of saying their own; and a child either tries risky jobs alone or gives up and waits instead of asking for help. Name the difference, and praise asking for help as a smart choice.","In the lab your child sorts foods into I like and No thanks, picks three toys and compares them with Pip's, sorts jobs into I can do it and Ask a grown-up, pours a drink up to a line, and makes an I like, Pip likes, I can card."]},"familyTip":"At snack time, take turns saying 'I like... but you like...'. Give your child one real job a day, like pouring their own water from a small jug or putting on their shoes, and wait before you step in.","keyIdea":"Your likes belong to you. You can say what you like and what you do not like, and that is OK. Friends like some things the same and some things different. You can do lots of jobs by yourself, and you ask a grown-up when a job is hot, fast or not safe.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Can you do it by yourself? Drag each job, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["💪 I can do it","🙋 Ask a grown-up"],"items":[["Wash my hands",0],["Put on my coat",0],["Tidy my toys",0],["Eat with a spoon",0],["Cross the road",1],["Use the oven",1],["Take medicine",1],["Use a sharp knife",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaglike","caption":"Flagship lab: sort foods you like, compare toys with a friend, sort jobs you can do, pour a drink and make an I like card.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_H4YZ1rSKP3":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is self-awareness?","choices":["Knowing your own feelings and habits","Being worried about yourself","Knowing what others think"],"answer":0,"hint":"Self means you. Awareness means knowing.","explain":"Self-awareness is knowing your own feelings and habits, like 'I go quiet when I am nervous'."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Sam spilled paint by accident. Tia's picture is ruined. What is the impact on Tia?","choices":["She is upset about her picture","Sam did not mean it","There is no impact"],"answer":0,"hint":"Impact is the effect on someone else, not what you meant.","explain":"Tia is upset. The impact is real even though it was an accident, so saying sorry still helps."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"'From my side it felt unfair, but I can see Tom's side too.' Which word fits?","choices":["Perspective","Trigger","Pattern"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word means how things look from one side?","explain":"Perspective. Each person sees the same event from their own side."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Leo was grumpy once after a spelling test. Is that a pattern?","choices":["No, it happened only once","Yes, any bad day is a pattern","Yes, because tests are hard"],"answer":0,"hint":"A pattern happens again and again.","explain":"No. One time is not a pattern. If it kept happening, it would be."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Mia noticed her cheeks felt hot {n} times today. What is the first step of self-reflection she has done?","choices":["She noticed","She made an assumption","She had an impact"],"answer":0,"hint":"Spotting something by paying attention has a name.","explain":"She noticed. Spotting hot cheeks {n} times is noticing; thinking about why comes next.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Ben is grumpy on {n} school days. Every time, he skipped breakfast. What is the trigger?","choices":["Skipping breakfast","Being grumpy","School"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for what came before the feeling every time.","explain":"Skipping breakfast came before every one of the {n} grumpy days, so it is the trigger.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"How can you check an assumption?","choices":["Ask the person","Keep guessing","Decide it must be true"],"answer":0,"hint":"An assumption has not been checked yet.","explain":"Ask the person. Checking turns a guess into something you know."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What does 'reflect' mean in 'reflect on your day'?","choices":["Think back on what happened and why","Look at yourself in a mirror","Forget about it"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is not about mirrors here. It is about your thinking.","explain":"To reflect is to think back on what happened and why. A mirror reflects light, but you reflect on your day."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Ella felt worried before a test on {n} days this month. What is this?","choices":["A pattern","A perspective","An impact"],"answer":0,"hint":"Something that happens again and again has a name.","explain":"A pattern. Worry before a test on {n} days is a repeat, so Ella can plan for it.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"'Jay did not sit with me, so he does not like me.' What is the part after 'so'?","choices":["An assumption","A fact I noticed","A trigger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did you check it, or guess it?","explain":"An assumption: a guess about what Jay thinks. You noticed he did not sit with you; you could ask him why."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Noah gets cross when he has slept less than {n} hours. What is the trigger?","choices":["Not enough sleep","Being cross","Noah"],"answer":0,"hint":"A trigger comes before the feeling and sets it off.","explain":"Not enough sleep (less than {n} hours) sets off the cross feeling, so it is the trigger.","params":{"n":[6,10]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some words help you talk about what goes on inside you. First you notice: you spot a feeling, like hot cheeks or a tight tummy. Then you reflect: you think back on what happened and why.","A trigger is the thing that sets a feeling off. Being hungry can be a trigger for feeling cross. If it happens again and again, that is a pattern. 'I notice a pattern: I get cross when I rush.'","An assumption is a guess you have not checked. 'Ava did not wave' is what you saw. 'Ava is cross with me' is an assumption. You can check it by asking her.","Impact is the effect you have on other people, even when you did not mean it. Perspective is how things look from one side. Knowing all this about yourself is called self-awareness."],"adult":["This vocabulary sits in PSHE and the IB PYP 'identity' strand. Pupils learn self-awareness, reflect, notice, pattern, trigger, assumption, impact and perspective, and use them about their own behaviour. Precise labels help children step back from a feeling and describe it, which supports self-regulation.","Three slips are common. Children hear 'reflect' as looking in a mirror rather than thinking back. They call one bad day a 'pattern' when a pattern needs repeats. And they mix up the trigger, which comes before a feeling, with the feeling itself.","The assumption versus fact distinction matters most for friendships. 'He did not sit with me' is noticed; 'he hates me' is assumed and can be checked. Likewise, intent and impact are both real: an accident can still hurt, and naming the impact helps repair it.","In the lab your child matches the words to their meanings, finds the trigger that comes before every grumpy morning in Leo's week, sorts noticed thoughts from assumptions, sees one paint spill from three perspectives, and drops choices into a pond to see their impact."]},"familyTip":"At dinner, each share one thing you noticed about your feelings today, and one trigger. Once a week, reflect together: is there a pattern? Model it yourself: 'I notice I get snappy when I am tired.'","keyIdea":"Precise words make your inner life easier to understand and talk about. To notice is to spot something by paying attention; to reflect is to think back on what happened and why. A trigger sets off a feeling, and a pattern is something that happens again and again. An assumption is a guess you have not checked. Impact is the effect your actions have on others, and perspective is how things look from one person's side. Put together, these build self-awareness: knowing your own feelings and habits.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each sentence something noticed, or an assumption? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["👀 Noticed","💭 Assumption"],"items":[["My hands were shaking.",0],["Kai looked away when I spoke.",0],["I got 4 out of 10.",0],["Mum used a quiet voice.",0],["Kai thinks I am boring.",1],["I am bad at maths.",1],["Mum is angry with me.",1],["Nobody likes my drawing.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagreflect","caption":"Flagship lab: match self-reflection words, find Leo's trigger in his week, sort noticed from assumed, see three perspectives, and test impact.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_rpug2tkYhb":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the difference between intent and impact?","choices":["Intent is what you meant; impact is how it felt to them","They mean the same thing","Impact is what you meant; intent is how they felt"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think: which one is inside your head, and which one happens to the other person?","explain":"Intent is what you meant to do. Impact is how it actually felt to the other person. They can be different."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is a clue about how someone feels?","choices":["Their face goes red and they look away","What you were thinking","The colour of their jumper"],"answer":0,"hint":"Clues come from the other person's face, body and words.","explain":"A red face and looking away are body-language clues. Your own thoughts are your intent, and a jumper tells you nothing about feelings."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Before you take the last slice of pizza, what should you do?","choices":["Think about how the others will feel, then ask","Take it quickly before anyone sees","Take it, because you were hungriest"],"answer":0,"hint":"This is about predicting the impact before you act.","explain":"Predicting how others will feel, then asking, stops an unfair impact before it happens."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Your friend says, \"That really hurt my feelings.\" What is the best reply?","choices":["\"I didn't know. I'm sorry it hurt.\"","\"I was only joking.\"","\"You're too sensitive.\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Which reply listens to the impact instead of arguing about intent?","explain":"Saying sorry for the hurt accepts the impact. 'Only joking' talks about your intent, and 'too sensitive' blames your friend."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Kai was telling you about his trip. You cut in to talk about yours, and he went quiet. Next time, what could you do differently?","choices":["Listen, then ask him a question about his trip","Talk louder so he hears you","Walk away"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what would make Kai feel heard.","explain":"Listening and asking a question shows Kai you care about his story. That is reflecting on what you could do differently."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Ben laughs when he sees his friend fall over. He thinks, \"It looked so funny.\" Which word describes Ben's thought?","choices":["Intent","Impact","Clue"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it what Ben meant, or what happened to his friend?","explain":"'It looked funny' is Ben's intent. The impact is how his friend felt, perhaps hurt or embarrassed."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"You shout \"Rubbish team!\" at the {n} players who lost. How many players could feel the impact of your words?","answer":"n","hint":"Your words were aimed at the whole team. Count every player.","explain":"All {n} players heard it, so all {n} could feel hurt. Words aimed at a group can have an impact on everyone in it.","params":{"n":[4,11]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"{n} children are waiting for the swing. You stay on and never give a turn. How many children miss out?","answer":"n","hint":"Everyone who is waiting misses out if you never take turns.","explain":"All {n} children miss out. Taking turns changes the impact for every one of them.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Why is \"I didn't mean it\" not enough on its own?","choices":["It talks about your intent, but the other person still felt the impact","Because you always mean it","Because saying sorry is never needed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about who still feels the hurt after you explain.","explain":"Explaining your intent can help, but the other person still felt the impact. Asking how they feel and putting it right matters too."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Zara says, \"Your hair looks funny today!\" She meant it as a joke. Lucy looks down and goes quiet. What was the impact?","choices":["Lucy felt hurt","Lucy felt it was funny","There was no impact because it was a joke"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at what Lucy did, not at what Zara meant.","explain":"Looking down and going quiet are clues that Lucy felt hurt. The intent was a joke, but the impact was hurt."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"At break you tell {n} friends that only you and one friend can play your game. Everyone else is left out. How many friends are left out?","answer":"n-1","hint":"One friend gets to play. Take that one away from {n}.","explain":"{n} friends take away the 1 who plays leaves {=n-1} left out. One choice can have an impact on lots of people at once.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You are not sure if your joke upset Tom. What is the best thing to do?","choices":["Ask, \"Was that okay?\" and listen","Say, \"You're fine, right?\"","Tell the joke again to check"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which choice lets Tom tell you how he feels?","explain":"Asking and listening lets Tom tell you the impact. Saying 'You're fine' tells him how to feel instead of finding out."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You borrow a pencil and keep it for {n} lessons. Your friend needs it in every lesson. In how many lessons did your friend go without it?","answer":"n","hint":"Your friend misses the pencil in every lesson you keep it.","explain":"Your friend went without it for {n} lessons. You may have meant to give it back, but the impact grew each lesson.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["When you do or say something, there are two sides. Your intent is what you meant. The impact is how it felt to the other person. You can mean well and still hurt someone, like laughing when a friend trips.","You cannot see inside someone's head, but you can look for clues. A face that goes red, someone going quiet, or a person walking away are clues that it landed badly. A smile, a laugh with you, or lots of questions are clues it landed well.","Your own feelings are not the clue. If you were laughing, that tells you how you felt, not how they felt. Watch them, not yourself.","Stop and think before you act. Ask yourself: how will this feel for them? If you are not sure afterwards, check in: 'Was that okay?' Then listen. Saying 'I didn't mean it' does not undo the impact, but asking and fixing it can help."],"adult":["This lesson teaches the gap between intent and impact. Intent is what a child meant. Impact is the effect the action really had on someone else. It sits in the social and emotional learning strands that CASEL calls self-awareness, social awareness and responsible decision-making. That last one includes thinking ahead about the consequences of your actions. In England it supports the statutory Relationships Education guidance (DfE, 2019) on caring friendships and respectful relationships. It also matches the PSHE Association Key Stage 2 aim of learning how words and actions affect others.","Key words for ages 8 to 9: intent, impact, consequence, perspective, empathy, and cues such as facial expression, body language and tone of voice. Children also learn to predict, reflect and check in. The skill has three parts. First, read the impact from cues after the event. Second, predict the impact before acting. Third, after a conflict, reflect on what you could have done differently.","Common mistakes: judging an action only by its intent ('I was only joking', so no harm was done). Reading your own feelings as theirs ('I was laughing, so it was fine'). Telling someone how they feel ('You're fine, right?') instead of asking. Blaming the other person for being hurt ('You're too sensitive'). Acting first and explaining later. A simple family routine helps: stop, predict, then check in.","In the lab your child drags a dot along an impact meter to show how an action landed, using a clue. Next they drag the real clue into a box, not the intent or a detail that tells you nothing. Then they test choices on the meter before acting. They rewind a story and replay a better move. Last, they pick a check-in that asks instead of tells. Every drag has a button twin."]},"familyTip":"When your child says 'I didn't mean it', agree first: 'I know you didn't.' Then ask: 'How do you think it felt for them? What clue tells you?' Model it yourself when you get something wrong: 'I meant to help, but I think I made you feel rushed.'","keyIdea":"Intent is what you meant to do. Impact is how your words or actions actually felt to the other person. They are not always the same: a joke you meant to be funny can still hurt. Read the clues in someone's face, body and words to see the impact. Before you act, predict how it will land. If you are not sure, check in and ask. After a problem, think about what you could do differently next time.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Intent or impact? Drag each one, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["intent: what I meant","impact: how it felt to them"],"items":[["I was only joking",0],["She went quiet",1],["I wanted to help",0],["He looked at the floor",1],["I didn't mean to",0],["They smiled at me",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagimpact","caption":"Flagship lab: read impact on a meter from clues, find the real clue, test choices before acting, rewind a story, check in.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Mb1JUJmnbX":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Your friends go quiet when you walk up. Which is another possible reason, not about you?","choices":["They had just finished a story","They were talking about me","They do not like me any more"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a reason that does not assume they were being unkind.","explain":"They may simply have finished a story. The other two choices repeat the same unkind guess."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is the best way to check what happened after Leo bumped your tray?","choices":["Did you mean to bump me?","Why did you do that on purpose?!","Bump him back later"],"answer":0,"hint":"A good check is a real question. It does not decide the answer for him.","explain":"Did you mean to? lets Leo explain. On purpose?! is an accusation, and bumping back acts on a guess."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Mia thinks: Ana always ignores me. Ana did not wave once today. What is wrong with Mia's thought?","choices":["One moment does not prove always","Nothing, it must be true","Mia should never think about it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the word always. How many times did it happen?","explain":"Always and never are signs of a hot thought. One missed wave is not a pattern."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Jo grabs your pen, laughs, says Mine now! and will not give it back for the third time this week. What fits the clues?","choices":["Jo probably meant it, so get help","It must be an accident","You can never decide anything"],"answer":0,"hint":"Questioning first thoughts means following the clues, not always saying accident.","explain":"Jo's words and the repeated grabbing are strong clues that it was on purpose. Tell an adult or ask firmly for it back."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Put these in the right order: Check, Notice the hot thought, Think of other reasons, Pause. Which comes first?","choices":["Notice the hot thought","Check","Think of other reasons"],"answer":0,"hint":"You have to spot the hot thought before you can slow it down.","explain":"Notice the hot thought first, then pause, then think of other reasons, then check."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Maya did not save you a seat. You never told her you wanted one. How sure can you be that she meant to leave you out?","choices":["Not sure: I cannot tell yet","Very sure she meant it","Very sure it was an accident"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do the clues point one way, or are they too thin to tell?","explain":"Maya may not have known. The clues do not show either way, so ask her before deciding."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"To pause, you take {s} slow breaths. Each breath takes 4 seconds. How many seconds does your pause last?","answer":"4*s","hint":"Multiply the number of breaths by 4 seconds.","explain":"{s} breaths × 4 seconds = {=4*s} seconds, long enough for the first hot feeling to settle.","params":{"s":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Rina lists {a} reasons why Kai might be quiet. {b} of them are unkind guesses about her. How many are other reasons?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Take the unkind guesses away from all the reasons.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} other reasons, so there is more than one way to explain why Kai is quiet.","params":{"a":[4,7],"b":[1,2]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which word means what someone meant to do?","choices":["intention","evidence","perspective"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is the hidden part you cannot see: what was in their mind.","explain":"Intention is what someone meant to do. Evidence is the clues, and perspective is how things look from where they stand."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Sam walked past Ella without saying hello. Which sentence is a fact, not a guess?","choices":["Sam did not say hello.","Sam is cross with Ella.","Sam thinks Ella is boring."],"answer":0,"hint":"A fact is something Ella could see or hear. A guess is about what is in Sam's head.","explain":"Ella saw that Sam did not say hello. Why he did not is a guess: he may not have seen her."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Your first thought is one possible reason. Then you think of {a} more reasons. How many possible reasons do you have now?","answer":"a+1","hint":"Count the first thought, then add the new reasons.","explain":"1 first thought + {a} more = {=a+1} possible reasons. The first thought is only one of them.","params":{"a":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Tom feels completely sure that Jas tripped him on purpose. What does that feeling prove?","choices":["Nothing yet: a feeling is not a clue","That Jas meant it","That Jas is a bully"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask: what did Tom see or hear, apart from his own feeling?","explain":"Feeling sure is not evidence. Tom needs clues, or he can ask Jas calmly what happened."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Imagine that in a crowded corridor, {b} out of every 10 bumps are accidents. Out of 10 bumps, how many are not accidents?","answer":"10-b","hint":"Take the accidents away from the 10 bumps.","explain":"10 − {b} = {=10-b} bumps are not accidents, so most bumps there are accidents. Assuming on purpose would usually be wrong.","params":{"b":[6,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Picture this. Leo bumps your tray and your lunch nearly falls. Your first thought is fast: he did that on purpose! But hold on. What did you actually see? You saw a bump. That is what happened. On purpose is a guess about why, because you cannot see inside Leo's head.","A first thought is just one idea. Try to find two more. Maybe the queue was crowded and someone pushed him. Maybe he tripped on a bag. Now you have three possible reasons, and you do not know yet which one is true.","Feeling very sure does not make a guess right. Clues do. Did he say oops? Was he carrying a lot? Some clues point to an accident. Some leave you unsure. And sometimes the clues show a person really did mean it, like someone who grabs your pen, laughs and will not give it back.","So, when a first thought makes you hot, use four steps. Notice the hot thought. Pause and breathe. Think of two other reasons. Then check: ask a calm question, such as Did you mean to bump me? A question lets the other person explain. Did you do that on purpose?! is blaming, not asking."],"adult":["This topic is about reading other people's intentions. Psychologists call one habit hostile attribution bias. It means assuming bad intent when a situation is unclear. Kenneth Dodge's research links it to more conflict. It also shows the habit can shrink when children learn to think of other reasons and check them. Therapists call a related thinking trap mind reading: treating a guess about what someone thinks as a fact.","The skills match PSHE and CASEL goals for ages 9 to 11. Children learn to tell what they saw from how they read it. They take another person's view, think of at least two other reasons and weigh the evidence. They also calm the first hot feeling and check their guess politely. Useful words: assumption, intention, evidence, interpretation and perspective.","Common mistakes: treating the first guess as fact; taking a strong feeling as proof; always and never thinking (he always does this) after one event; checking with a loaded question (why did you do that on purpose?) that is really blame; and acting on the guess with revenge or gossip. Do not overcorrect, though. The aim is not to call everything an accident. When the clues show real unkindness, a child should see it and get help.","In the lab your child sorts sentences into what happened and a guess about why. They pick two other reasons for three puzzling moments. They open clue cards and drag a sure-o-meter to accident, can't tell yet or on purpose. Then they put notice, pause, think and check in order and drag calm words onto a story. Every drag has a button twin."]},"familyTip":"Next time your child says someone was mean, ask two questions: what did you actually see, and what else could explain it? Aim for two other reasons before deciding. If the clues still point to unkindness, take it seriously.","keyIdea":"When something puzzling happens, split what you saw from your guess about why. Leo bumped my tray is what happened; he did it on purpose is a guess about his intention. A first thought is only one possible reason, so think of at least two others: the queue was crowded, he tripped. Feeling sure is not evidence. Pause, look for clues, and check with a calm question such as Did you mean to? Follow the clues either way: sometimes they show it was an accident, sometimes you cannot tell yet, and sometimes someone really did mean it.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What happened, or a guess about why? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["👀 What happened","💭 A guess about why"],"items":[["Kai did not reply to my message.",0],["Kai is angry with me.",1],["Mo stepped on my foot in the corridor.",0],["Mo wanted to hurt me.",1],["Nia sat at a different table today.",0],["Nia does not want to be my friend.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmaybe","caption":"Flagship lab: split fact from guess, find other reasons, weigh clues on a sure-o-meter, order pause-think-check and choose words that check, not blame.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_TU3BcLOgiV":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Is it OK to feel angry?","choices":["Yes, every feeling is OK","No, angry is a bad feeling","Only for grown-ups"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about feelings and doings. Which part can be not OK?","explain":"All feelings are OK, angry too. It is a doing, like hitting, that can be not OK."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Jo says, \"I was so cross, I had to kick him.\" What is wrong with this?","choices":["Being cross does not make you kick","Nothing, the feeling made him do it","Jo should not feel cross"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does a feeling choose your doing for you?","explain":"A feeling does not choose for you. Jo could stop, breathe and pick a doing that does not hurt."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which one is a doing?","choices":["Stamping your feet","Worried","Jealous"],"answer":0,"hint":"A doing is something your body does.","explain":"Stamping is something your body does. Worried and jealous are feelings inside you."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A big dog barks and Kit feels scared. What should Kit do first?","choices":["Stop and hold a grown-up's hand","Throw a stick at the dog","Run into the road"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which choice keeps everyone safe?","explain":"Feeling scared is OK. Stopping and holding a grown-up's hand is a safe doing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What is the gap?","choices":["The time between a feeling and a doing","A feeling that goes away","A doing that hurts"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of STOP in the lab.","explain":"The gap is the time between the feeling and the doing. Stop and breathe in it, then choose."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Max lost a game and feels sad. Which is a kind doing?","choices":["Tell a friend \"I feel sad\"","Throw the game on the floor","Say the winner cheated"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which doing shows the feeling without hurting anyone?","explain":"Saying the feeling in words is a kind doing. Throwing things or blaming hurts."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Lily feels {a} big feelings today. Each time, she stops and picks one kind doing. How many kind doings does she pick?","answer":"a","hint":"One kind doing for each big feeling.","explain":"{a} feelings, one kind doing each, makes {a} kind doings.","params":{"a":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Sam feels jealous {j} times this week. How many of those times was Sam bad just for feeling jealous?","answer":"0","hint":"Is a feeling ever bad on its own?","explain":"The answer is 0. Feeling jealous {j} times is OK. Only a doing can be not OK.","params":{"j":[2,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which one is a feeling?","choices":["Scared","Shouting","Hiding"],"answer":0,"hint":"A feeling happens inside you. A doing is what your body does.","explain":"Scared is a feeling inside you. Shouting and hiding are doings: things your body does."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Ben feels cross. He stops and counts {n} slow breaths before he chooses. He has done {k} so far. How many more breaths?","answer":"n-k","where":"k<n","hint":"Start at {n} breaths and take away the {k} he has done.","explain":"{n} take away {k} is {=n-k}. Those slow breaths make a gap, so Ben can choose a kind doing.","params":{"n":[3,6],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Ava feels jealous of her friend's new doll. What is a kind doing?","choices":["Say \"I like your doll. Can we play?\"","Grab the doll","Say the doll is ugly"],"answer":0,"hint":"Feeling jealous is OK. Which doing does not hurt her friend?","explain":"Ava can feel jealous and still choose a kind doing, like asking to play together."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A box has {f} feeling cards and {d} doing cards. How many cards are there in all?","answer":"f+d","hint":"Add the feeling cards and the doing cards.","explain":"{f} and {d} make {=f+d} cards. Each one is either a feeling inside you or a doing your body does.","params":{"f":[2,6],"d":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A feeling happens inside you. Cross, scared, sad and jealous are feelings. You cannot always stop a feeling from coming.","A doing is what your body does. Stamping, hiding, hugging and shouting are doings. You get to choose your doings.","Every feeling is OK. Feeling jealous does not make you bad. But some doings are not OK, like hitting or breaking things.","A big feeling does not have to win. Stop. Take a big breath. Then pick a kind doing, like saying \"I feel cross!\" or asking a grown-up for help."],"adult":["This topic sits in self-regulation. In England it links to the DfE Health Education guidance (2019) at Key Stage 1, which asks pupils to recognise and talk about feelings and to manage them in ways that are right for the situation. It also builds on the EYFS goal 'Managing Self'. Between about 6 and 8, children move from acting straight away on a feeling (impulse) to holding a short pause and choosing a response.","The key words are emotion (or feeling), action (or behaviour) and response. A trigger sets off the feeling. The feeling is not a choice and is never wrong. The action is the part a child can learn to choose. A short pause, such as stop and breathe, is the first self-regulation tool. It gives the thinking part of the brain time to catch up with a strong feeling.","Common mistakes: telling a child a feeling is bad (\"Don't be jealous!\"), which teaches them to hide feelings rather than manage actions. Saying \"he made me do it\" or \"I was too angry to stop\", as if the feeling chose the action. Mixing up a feeling with its action, for example calling shouting or crying a feeling. Be careful to praise the choice, such as \"You were really cross and you still kept your hands to yourself\".","In the lab your child sorts feelings from doings, finds three kind doings for one cross feeling, drags a STOP sign into the gap before choosing, and judges cards as OK or not OK. The rule they find is that feelings are always OK and doings are chosen."]},"familyTip":"When your child has a big feeling, name it and then the choice: \"You feel cross. Hitting is not OK. You can stamp your feet or tell me.\" Later, ask: what did you feel, and what did you choose to do?","keyIdea":"A feeling, like cross, scared or jealous, happens inside you. An action (a doing) is what your body does, like hitting, hiding or saying \"I feel cross\". Every feeling is OK, but not every action is OK. A big feeling does not make you do anything: there is a gap between the feeling and the doing. Stop and breathe in the gap, then choose a doing that does not hurt anyone.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Feeling or doing? Drag each card to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Feeling (inside me)","Doing (my body does it)"],"items":[["Sad",0],["Crying",1],["Worried",0],["Running away",1],["Happy",0],["Hugging",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfeelact","caption":"Flagship lab: sort feelings from doings, find kind doings for one cross feeling, put a STOP in the gap, and judge what is OK.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_bkMDDstwwG":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the fairest way to see if your drawing has improved?","choices":["Compare it with your drawing from last year","Compare it with the best artist in class","Ask if it is the best in the room"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who should you compare with to see your own change?","explain":"Your old drawing shows where you started. Other people started somewhere else."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Jas’s reading scores this year: 20, 24, 22, 30. What is true?","choices":["She improved, with one dip","She is getting worse","She has not changed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first and last scores, then at the dip.","explain":"20 to 30 is real growth. The drop to 22 was one dip, not the whole line."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Rosa can reach the top shelf now and could not at six. What caused it?","choices":["Growing up","Practice","A new strategy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you practise being taller?","explain":"She grew. Some change comes from growing up, not effort."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Kai kept forgetting his PE kit. Now he packs it the night before. What changed?","choices":["He used a new strategy","He grew taller","Someone else packs it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did he do the same thing more, or do it a different way?","explain":"Packing the night before is a new way of doing it, a strategy."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is the strongest evidence that you have grown as a writer?","choices":["Your story from Year 3 next to one from this year","Feeling cleverer today","A friend saying you are good"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one can you actually look at and compare?","explain":"Two pieces of your work side by side show the change. Feelings and praise do not."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Mia says, “I’m bad at maths and always will be.” What would looking back show her?","choices":["Things she found hard before got easier","She is right to give up","Maths ability is fixed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Was there a time she could not add or count?","explain":"She once found counting hard and now does not. That is evidence the hard parts now can change too."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Ben could do {a} press-ups in September. In June he can do {b}. How many more can he do?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Take the September number away from the June number.","explain":"{b} take away {a} is {=b-a} more. That is growth compared with past Ben.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[10,20]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Ella practised spelling for {m} minutes a day for {d} days. How many minutes of practice is that?","answer":"m*d","hint":"Multiply minutes per day by the number of days.","explain":"{m} minutes times {d} days is {=m*d} minutes. Small daily practice adds up.","params":{"m":[5,15],"d":[10,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which question helps you most after you get better at something?","choices":["What helped me improve?","Am I better than everyone?","Was it just luck?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which question tells you what to do next time?","explain":"Naming what helped (practice, help, a strategy) tells you what to use for the next hard thing."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which sentence shows growth thinking?","choices":["I can’t swim a length yet.","I’m just not sporty.","Swimming is for other people."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a sentence that leaves room to change.","explain":"“Yet” says it can still change. The other two say you never can."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Last year Priya could juggle {a} times in a row. Now she can juggle {b} more than that. How many in a row now?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Start with last year and add the improvement.","explain":"{a} plus {b} more is {=a+b} in a row. Comparing with last year shows her growth.","params":{"a":[2,8],"b":[3,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Leo can ride a bike now because his sister held the seat until he could balance. What made the change?","choices":["Help from someone","Growing up","He was born good at it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who did something in the story?","explain":"His sister held the seat. That is help from someone."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Sam’s first story was {a} lines long. His newest is {k} times as long. How many lines is his newest story?","answer":"a*k","hint":"Multiply the first length by how many times longer it is.","explain":"{a} lines times {k} is {=a*k} lines. Old work is real evidence of growth.","params":{"a":[5,12],"k":[2,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Think of something that was hard when you were six. Maybe tying your laces, reading a chapter book, or staying calm when you were cross. Now some of those are easy. You did not notice it happening, but you changed.","That is useful to know. If hard things got easier before, the things that are hard now can get easier too. Saying “I can’t do it yet” is not just being positive. When you look back, it is simply true.","Change has causes. Usually it is practice, help from someone, or a new strategy, like writing a list so you stop forgetting. Some change is just growing up: you cannot practise being taller. Naming what helped tells you what to try next time.","Be fair to yourself. Compare yourself with you a year ago, not with the best person in the class. Expect dips: one bad week does not wipe out a year of growth. Keep old work, because a story from last year is better evidence than a feeling."],"adult":["The PSHE Association plan for Key Stage 2 asks pupils to know their own strengths, skills and achievements, and to cope with setbacks by re-framing unhelpful thoughts. CASEL lists a growth mindset as part of self-awareness. This lesson makes it concrete. Pupils look back at real change in themselves, instead of being told to think positively.","The key move is to judge progress against your own past, not against classmates. The second move is to name the cause. Practice, help from an adult or friend, a new strategy and simply growing up all bring change. Telling them apart shows a child what to try next. Studies of mindset find the message works best with good strategies and support, not effort alone.","Common mistakes: comparing only with the strongest child in the class, reading one dip as proof of no progress, praising effort that is not working instead of suggesting a new strategy, thinking all growth comes from effort when some is just growing up, and trusting a feeling of being bad at something over evidence such as old work.","In the lab your child puts then-and-now cards on a timeline, names the cause of four changes, sorts fixed from growth sentences, and picks fair evidence, including a line graph with a dip. Every drag has a button twin."]},"familyTip":"Dig out something your child made or wrote a year or two ago and put it next to something recent. Ask: “What can you do now that you couldn’t then? What helped?” Share one of your own then-and-now stories too.","keyIdea":"You keep changing as you grow. Things that were hard at six are easy now, so the things that are hard now are not fixed either. To see your growth, compare yourself with past you, not with other people. Name what made each change: practice, help from someone, a new strategy, or simply growing up. A dip is part of the line, and a record of old work is better evidence than a feeling.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What made the change? Drag each story to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Practice","Help from someone","A new strategy","Growing up"],"items":[["Played piano scales every day for months",0],["A coach showed her how to hold the bat",1],["Used a timer to stop putting off homework",2],["Can now reach the light switch",3],["Read aloud to his gran each night",0],["Her dad sat with her for her first sleepover away",1],["Started checking answers by working backwards",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagthennow","caption":"Flagship lab: put then-and-now cards on a timeline, name what caused each change, sort fixed from growth thinking, and choose fair evidence.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
